## workbuddy_cloud_service
Inspect or activate the WorkBuddy Cloud Service for the app in `directory` — a managed Serverless backend providing Database, end-user Auth, file Storage and a keyless LLM API. Set `action` to "inspect" (default, read-only) or "activate".
PAIRED SKILL — READ IT BEFORE WRITING CODE: this tool owns only the environment lifecycle (inspect / activate). The "cloud-service" skill owns everything else: which SDK to install (npm package or CDN script), how to initialize the client from `publicConfig`, and the per-module API signatures in `references/<module>/code-generation.md` (database / auth / storage / llm), plus `references/<module>/management.md` for inspecting existing resources. Load that skill via the Skill tool and follow it — do NOT write the integration from memory. The SDK is NOT `@cloudbase/js-sdk`, NOT supabase's `createClient(url, anonKey)`, and hand-written `fetch` against `/.cloud/**` is wrong. Read only the module docs the current request touches.
WHEN TO USE: the app must persist data across reloads, needs sign-up/login or per-user data, needs to upload files or produce accessible file links, or needs to call a large model without the user supplying an API key. Also when the user mentions 云服务 / 开通云服务 / 后端 / 数据库 / 登录注册 / 文件上传 / AI 能力, asks to inspect or maintain existing tables/users/files/model usage, or reports that saving data, logging in, uploading or model calls fail. Do NOT use for a purely static page, for front-end-only interactions with no persistence, or when the user already picked another backend (their own service, Supabase, …).
ONE ENVIRONMENT PER APP: all four modules share a single environment, so never activate per module. The environment belongs to the app at `directory`, not to a global account, and data is never shared across apps.
INSPECT FIRST — mandatory order: (1) call action "inspect" to list the applications registered in this session; it reports each application's REAL cloud-service status checked against the backend (already ACTIVE / NOT enabled / previously destroyed / unknown) — a local manifest entry does NOT by itself mean the cloud service is on, nor off. (2) decide which application this request belongs to (see MULTIPLE APPLICATIONS below) and call "activate" with the right applicationMode; for an application already ACTIVE, do NOT tell the user to re-enable cloud service — just call "activate" with applicationMode "reuse" to fetch its publicConfig; it reuses the existing binding and will NOT prompt the user again. (3) only write cloud-dependent code AFTER activation is confirmed successful — never code against an environment you have not confirmed.
MULTIPLE APPLICATIONS — a session may hold several applications (each becomes one when its cloud service is activated, or when it is published as a Site). You MUST say which one to bind: pass applicationMode "reuse" + applicationId to keep building an existing application, or applicationMode "create" + appName to start a new independent one. Decide by context (e.g. "keep improving the expense tracker" → reuse; "start another project" → create). If it is genuinely unclear which application the request targets, you MAY ask the user a plain attribution question (which app), or the tool returns a "cloud_service_application_selection_required" result carrying the candidate list — pick one and call again. Do NOT default to the first application, and do NOT guess. (Asking WHICH application is allowed and is different from asking WHETHER to enable cloud service, which you must never ask — see below.) With an empty session, applicationMode may be omitted and is treated as "create". Binding is idempotent and never double-charges, so re-activating a reused application to ensure it is on is safe.
PUBLISHING AFTER ACTIVATION — activating and publishing are SEPARATE steps: activation provisions the backend environment and reserves the app's share domain, it does NOT put anything online. To publish the app online later, call `workbuddy_sites_deploy` and REUSE this application's `applicationId` as its `appId`. Reusing the id keeps the reserved domain (and therefore the cloud-service Origin) intact; a brand-new app gets a different domain and the app's cloud login breaks, because the server enforces an exact Origin match. The `applicationId` here is the SAME id the sites tool uses — there is ONE application identity across cloud service and publishing.
MINI PROGRAMS — pass appType "miniprogram" whenever activating cloud service for a WeChat mini program, for both applicationMode "create" and "reuse". On create it records the application type; on reuse it selects mini-program-only local identity checks without rewriting the cloud record. Omitting it defaults create to "web" and keeps reuse on the ordinary web path. Decide it from the user's request, not from files on disk — at activation time a from-scratch mini program has none yet.
ACTIVATION CONFIRMATION IS UI-DRIVEN — NEVER ASK IT YOURSELF: activation provisions a real resource that may carry quota/billing and always needs the user's go-ahead, but that go-ahead is collected by the trusted WorkBuddy UI, NOT by you. The moment you determine the app needs a backend, call action "activate" directly; the tool then makes the WorkBuddy UI pop a confirmation dialog and blocks until the user decides. NEVER use AskUserQuestion, and never ask a natural-language question (e.g. "要不要开通云服务？"), to decide whether to activate — doing so bypasses the trusted dialog (it never pops) and grants/denies a billable resource on the user's behalf. Just activate and let the UI confirm; only refrain from activating when the app plainly does not need a backend.
STATUS SEMANTICS — on success `billingStatus` is "normal" (usable) and `provisionStatus` is "assigned" (bound and ready). Binding is served from a warm pool and returns in seconds; there is no "provisioning-in-progress" state to poll.
WHEN BINDING CANNOT SUCCEED RIGHT NOW the result comes back as `cloud_service_unavailable` (see CAPABILITY BOUNDARY): pool temporarily empty → a "try again later" message (the user can retry; do NOT loop); account app-quota used up → a message plus `useLocalImplementation: true` (continue locally, see below); backend busy → a "try again later" message.
CONFIRMATION IS NOT YOURS TO FAKE: the confirmation for activate is raised by the trusted WorkBuddy UI. This tool REJECTS `confirmed` / `confirm` / `skipConfirm` / `force` — passing them means the model is granting itself the user's authorization. Equally, do NOT substitute your own question for it: never use AskUserQuestion or a chat prompt to ask whether to activate — that both skips the trusted dialog (it never pops) and quietly self-authorizes. If the result carries `declinedByUser: true`, the user said no: that is a NORMAL outcome, not an error. Report it and stop; do not re-ask in a loop or route around it.
CAPABILITY BOUNDARY — the result may be `{"type":"cloud_service_unavailable"}` carrying a ready-to-use `userMessage` (plus an internal `agentGuidance` that is for you, not for the user). Relay `userMessage` to the user (translated into the reply language, keeping every point it makes). Do NOT invent another cloud-service tool name, and do NOT hit the cloud-service backend directly with Bash/curl. If the result also carries `useLocalImplementation: true` (e.g. the account app-quota is used up), that is your explicit signal to CONTINUE WITH A LOCAL IMPLEMENTATION right away — do NOT ask the user whether to, and do NOT stop; otherwise (no such flag) relay the message and STOP without retrying.
NO SILENT DOWNGRADE (unless told to): never fall back to localStorage / an in-memory array / a mock JSON file just to make the feature "work", and never fabricate an environment id or config. That reads as if the request was fulfilled while nothing is actually persisted. The ONLY exception is an unavailable result that carries `useLocalImplementation: true` — then a local fallback is explicitly sanctioned. Otherwise, if a local-storage-only version might genuinely help, ASK the user first and wait for an explicit yes. (This is a DIFFERENT decision from activation — choosing a local fallback has no trusted UI dialog, so asking here is correct; the "never ask, just activate" rule applies only to gating activate itself.)
ON SUCCESS the result carries `publicConfig` with `resourceId` / `endpoint` / `oauthRelayBaseUrl` / `publishableKey` — the only values safe to ship inside the app's front-end code. `oauthRelayBaseUrl` is required to initialize a web app that offers WeChat QR sign-in; omit it and `signInWithOAuth` fails at runtime. `publishableKey` identifies the app and carries no permissions by itself (the server enforces an exact Origin match), so it may live in app source, but keep it out of logs. The underlying environment id and provider keys are server-side only and are never returned; if a pattern requires the front end to hold a long-lived key, the pattern is wrong.

